G15 / Route B patch 0013 — the negative control's supply side.
==============================================================

ONE CONCEPTUAL CHANGE: make a stale supplied offset REACHABLE again, so `0012`'s
demotion can be tested instead of asserted.

WHY. `0012` deleted the `0xd4a8` / "RouteBThing.value" special case — correctly;
it was a live landmine. But that special case was the ONLY path that ever passed
a non-zero `pool_offset`, and the runtime gates the assertion on
`pool_offset != 0` (`runtime/lib/object.cc:1257`). So after `0012` the demoted
assertion path was unreachable from the embedder: one call site, argument a
literal `0`, no env var, no second caller — verified, not assumed.

That makes `0012`'s own owed control unrunnable:

    "feed the historical 0xd4a8 as a supplied offset where it points at a VALID
     BUT DIFFERENT object, and require the assertion path to report
     POOL_ENTRY_NOT_FUNCTION or pool_entry_equals_target=0 — never a confident
     identity — while the DERIVED scan independently reports the true location"

Without it, `next_cycle_precommit.md` claim 1 can return rows 1 and 2 only, which
that document requires be reported as PARTIAL and not as "instrumentation works".
Row 3 is the only row whose PASS is a negative result, and an arm that can only
come back green is the failure mode this project has named five times.

WHAT CHANGED — ONE FILE, `shell/common/shorebird/shorebird.cc`

  1. A default supplied offset of `0xd4a8`, applied to EVERY target rather than
     to one hard-coded selector. It is the value the control is specified
     against. It is NOT the old constant restored: nothing derives an identity
     from it (the derived `tpool_*` scan is the producer and is untouched), it
     is not keyed on a fixture's selector, and removing it costs only this block.

  2. A per-run override, read from `<active patch path>.assert_pool_offset`
     beside the patch. Base-0 parse, so `0x...` or decimal. This exists because
     whether `0xd4a8` lands IN RANGE in a release that does not exist yet is not
     knowable in advance — and if it does not, the assertion reports
     INDEX_OUT_OF_RANGE, which is NEITHER of row 3's two named outcomes. The
     override lets a valid-but-different slot be chosen after the release is cut,
     ON THE SAME BUILD, instead of costing a second full iOS engine build.

  3. A parse failure is REPORTED and the default stands — never absorbed. Two
     independent receipts make a misread sidecar unmistakable: a `RouteBReport`
     line, and the trace's existing `pool_offset=` field, which records what was
     ACTUALLY supplied rather than what was intended.

WHAT IS UNTOUCHED, AND CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
---------------------------------------------------
`runtime/lib/object.cc` and `runtime/include/dart_route_b_trace.h` are byte-identical
to their `0012` capture. The assertion path was READ before this change to confirm
it is pure-read — it writes `trace->` fields only and never touches the target or
the attach — so supplying an offset cannot perturb the attach being measured. It
also runs AFTER the derived scan, so both instruments report in the SAME trace,
which is exactly what row 3 requires.

The six-state CALLER scan stays unrequested (`caller_name=nullptr`) and
unconflated. Two instruments, still two.

